const unit14={
id:14,title:'Nutrition across the life cycle',headline:'Grow with purpose.<br>Age with strength.',intro:'Connect development, pregnancy, lactation, infant and child growth, adolescence and healthy aging across 12 life-cycle topics.',quizNote:'All 30 source questions are included. Questions 17 and 27 are discussion items because energy needs vary and a fixed age-related calorie decline is not a universal rule.',
lessons:[
['Cell growth and development','Separate more cells from larger cells.','<p><b>Hyperplasia</b> is an increase in the number of cells. <b>Hypertrophy</b> is an increase in the size of existing cells. Both contribute to normal growth, but their relative importance changes by tissue and life stage.</p><p>A <b>teratogen</b> is an exposure that can disrupt embryonic or fetal development. Risk depends on dose, timing, duration, genetics and the developing organ system.</p><div class="example"><b>Memory line</b><p>“Plasia” changes cell population; “trophy” changes cell size.</p></div>'],
['Pregnancy timing and birth outcomes','Use gestational age and size as separate measurements.','<p>The embryonic period occupies roughly weeks 3 through 8 after conception, when major organs begin forming. The fetal period follows and emphasizes growth and maturation. The first trimester includes the entire embryonic period and part of the fetal period.</p><p><b>Preterm</b> describes birth before 37 completed weeks. <b>Low birth weight</b> means less than 2,500 g (5 lb 8 oz), regardless of gestational age. A baby can therefore be full term and low birth weight.</p><div class="example"><b>Two questions</b><p>Ask “How early?” for gestational age and “How small?” for birth weight.</p></div>'],
['Healthy pregnancy weight gain','Match the range to prepregnancy BMI.','<p>For a singleton pregnancy, recommended total gain depends on prepregnancy BMI. A person beginning pregnancy in the healthy BMI range is generally advised to gain <b>25–35 lb</b>. Lower ranges apply with overweight or obesity, and a higher range applies when underweight.</p><p>These are population guides. Fetal growth, fluid status, nausea, diabetes, multiple gestation and other clinical factors can change the plan.</p><div class="example"><b>Pattern over perfection</b><p>Monitor the trajectory with prenatal care rather than judging a single week.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.acog.org/womens-health/experts-and-stories/ask-acog/how-much-weight-should-i-gain-during-pregnancy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACOG: pregnancy weight gain</a></p>'],
['Pregnancy energy and macronutrients','Add enough energy without “eating for two.”','<p>The pregnancy carbohydrate RDA is <b>175 g/day</b>, supporting maternal metabolism and fetal glucose needs. The protein RDA is about <b>71 g/day</b> for many adults during pregnancy, though individualized needs depend on body size and clinical context.</p><p>Energy needs rise gradually. The Dietary Guidelines estimate about <b>+340 kcal/day</b> in the second trimester and <b>+452 kcal/day</b> in the third for many adults. These are averages, not meal prescriptions.</p><div class="example"><b>Source calculations</b><p>From 25 g carbohydrate, reaching 175 g adds 150 g. From 150 g protein, reducing to about 71 g removes roughly 80 g.</p></div>'],
['Essential fats and prenatal micronutrients','Build quality with omega-3s, folate and clinical care.','<p>Essential fatty-acid sources should appear regularly—several times each week. Canola oil supplies alpha-linolenic acid (ALA); walnuts, flax and chia also contribute. Low-mercury fish can supply DHA and EPA.</p><p>People capable of becoming pregnant are advised to obtain <b>400 mcg/day folic acid</b> from supplements and/or fortified foods in addition to food folate. Pregnancy needs are expressed as 600 mcg dietary folate equivalents.</p><p>Smoking during pregnancy reduces oxygen delivery and blood flow to the fetus and raises risks including impaired growth and preterm birth. Support for quitting is a core prenatal intervention.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Pregnancy-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: pregnancy nutrition</a></p>'],
['Colostrum and human milk','Follow immune protection from first milk onward.','<p><b>Colostrum</b> is the concentrated early milk produced after birth. It is rich in immune factors and tailored to the newborn’s small stomach.</p><p><b>Lactoferrin</b> binds iron, supports its handling and limits iron availability to some microbes. The principal carbohydrate is lactose, accompanied by a diverse set of <b>human milk oligosaccharides</b> that help shape the microbiome and block pathogen attachment.</p><div class="example"><b>Food and defence</b><p>Human milk supplies nutrients while also delivering cells, antibodies and bioactive molecules.</p></div>'],
['Lactation energy needs','Use a range instead of one automatic total.','<p>Well-nourished breastfeeding mothers generally need about <b>330–400 additional kcal/day</b> compared with prepregnancy intake. Needs vary with body size, activity, milk production, exclusivity of breastfeeding and postpartum weight change.</p><p>For someone consuming 2,400 kcal before pregnancy, a general estimate is roughly 2,730–2,800 kcal/day. Appetite, growth monitoring and individualized clinical guidance matter more than a single test number.</p><div class="example"><b>Why the quiz item is unscored</b><p>Both 2,730 and 2,800 fit current guidance, depending on which end of the recommended range is used.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/breastfeeding-special-circumstances/hcp/diet-micronutrients/maternal-diet.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC: maternal diet during breastfeeding</a></p>'],
['Infant growth, vitamin D and iron','Support rapid growth with timely supplementation and foods.','<p>Many healthy infants double birth weight by about <b>4–6 months</b> and triple it by around the first birthday, although individual trajectories vary.</p><p>Infants receiving human milk need <b>400 IU (10 mcg) vitamin D daily</b> beginning soon after birth. Formula-fed infants need supplementation until intake reaches about 32 oz (1 L) of vitamin-D-fortified formula per day.</p><p>Full-term infants are born with iron stores that often support the first four to six months. Around six months, iron-rich complementary foods become important; preterm infants may need a different plan.</p><p><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/nutrition-report/media/pdfs/2026/06/Fat-Soluble-Vitamin-D-508.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC: infant vitamin D</a></p>'],
['Growth assessment after age two','Interpret BMI by age and sex, not as a diagnosis.','<p>For children age 2 years and older, clinicians commonly calculate <b>BMI-for-age</b> and plot it on sex-specific growth charts. Classification depends on age and sex because children are growing.</p><table><tr><th>Percentile</th><th>Current CDC category</th></tr><tr><td>&lt;5th</td><td>Underweight</td></tr><tr><td>5th to &lt;85th</td><td>Healthy weight</td></tr><tr><td>85th to &lt;95th</td><td>Overweight</td></tr><tr><td>≥95th</td><td>Obesity</td></tr></table><p>BMI is a screening measure, not a complete diagnosis; growth trajectory and clinical context matter.</p><p><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/growth-chart-training/hcp/using-bmi/screening-measure.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC: BMI-for-age screening</a></p>'],
['Childhood iron, iodine and cognition','Protect growth and learning during rapid development.','<p>Iron deficiency anemia in children can cause fatigue, pallor, irritability, inattentiveness and reduced appetite. Severe or prolonged deficiency can affect cognitive and behavioral development.</p><p>Iodine is required for thyroid hormone and neurodevelopment. Deficiency during pregnancy, infancy or childhood can impair both growth and cognition.</p><p>Because appetite, attention and development have many causes, suspected deficiency should be assessed rather than diagnosed from symptoms alone.</p><div class="example"><b>Source “except” item</b><p>Increased appetite is not the expected sign; decreased appetite can occur.</p></div>'],
['Adolescence and peak bone building','Use the growth spurt to strengthen bone.','<p>Adolescence is a major period of bone mineral accrual. The calcium RDA for ages 9–18 is <b>1,300 mg/day</b>, supported by vitamin D, adequate energy and protein, and weight-bearing activity.</p><p>Dairy foods, fortified alternatives, calcium-set tofu, canned fish with bones and lower-oxalate greens can contribute. Restrictive eating, low energy availability and missed menstrual periods can compromise bone development.</p><div class="example"><b>Build the bank</b><p>Peak bone mass is shaped by genetics, hormones, nutrition and mechanical loading over many years.</p></div>'],
['Healthy aging and nutrient density','Preserve muscle, bone and absorption as needs change.','<p>With aging, lean mass and often resting energy expenditure decline, while body-fat proportion tends to rise. Total calorie needs may fall, but needs for protein and most micronutrients do not fall proportionally. Nutrient-dense foods and resistance activity become especially valuable.</p><p>Food-bound vitamin B12 absorption can be reduced by atrophic gastritis, low stomach acid or acid-suppressing medication, all more common with age. Crystalline B12 in fortified foods or supplements can remain well absorbed.</p><p>Soy <b>isoflavones</b> are structurally similar to estrogen and can bind estrogen receptors, but their effects are weaker and tissue-specific.</p><div class="example"><b>No fixed calorie countdown</b><p>A “10 kcal per year” decline is a textbook estimate, not an individual prescription. Activity and body composition are major determinants.</p></div><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB12-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: vitamin B12 and aging</a></p>']
],
questions:[
{n:1,q:'An increase in the number of cells is called:',o:['Hyperplasia','Hypoplasia','Hypertrophy','Hypotrophy'],a:0,e:'Hyperplasia means an increase in cell number.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:'An increase in cell size is called:',o:['Hyperplasia','Hypoplasia','Hypertrophy','Hypotrophy'],a:2,e:'Hypertrophy means enlargement of existing cells.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:'Substances that can disrupt normal embryonic or fetal development are called:',o:['Anatogens','Caratogens','Teratogens','Seratogens'],a:2,e:'Teratogens are exposures capable of disturbing prenatal development; risk depends strongly on timing and dose.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'The first trimester includes:',o:['The entire embryonic period only','The entire embryonic period and part of the fetal period','The entire embryonic and fetal periods','The entire fetal period'],a:1,e:'The embryonic period ends early in pregnancy; the first trimester continues into the fetal period.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'A baby born at 38 weeks weighing 3 lb 8 oz is:',o:['Low birth weight','Premature','Normal for gestational age','Large for gestational age'],a:0,e:'Thirty-eight weeks is term, but 3 lb 8 oz is below the 2,500 g low-birth-weight threshold.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'Recommended total gain for a singleton pregnancy beginning at a healthy BMI is generally:',o:['15–25 lb','20–35 lb','25–35 lb','28–40 lb'],a:2,e:'The standard population range is 25–35 lb for a healthy prepregnancy BMI; individual care may differ.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'A pregnant person eating 25 g carbohydrate daily would add how much to reach the 175 g pregnancy RDA?',o:['100 g','125 g','150 g','175 g'],a:2,e:'175 minus 25 equals 150 g additional carbohydrate.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'About how many additional kcal/day are recommended in the third trimester?',o:['150','250','350','450'],a:3,e:'Current Dietary Guidelines estimate about 452 additional kcal/day in the third trimester, commonly rounded to 450.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'From 150 g protein/day, about how much could be removed while still approximating the 71 g pregnancy RDA?',o:['20 g','40 g','60 g','80 g'],a:3,e:'150 minus 71 is 79 g, which rounds to 80 g. Individual protein needs can vary.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'How often should foods rich in essential fatty acids generally be included during pregnancy?',o:['Once per week','Several times per week','Several times per month','Once per month'],a:1,e:'Regular inclusion several times weekly supports essential-fat intake and dietary variety.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Which listed oil is a meaningful source of the omega-3 fatty acid ALA?',o:['Olive oil','Corn oil','Canola oil','Peanut oil'],a:2,e:'Canola oil supplies more alpha-linolenic acid than the other listed oils.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:12,q:'How much folic acid should a person capable of pregnancy obtain daily from supplements or fortified foods in addition to food folate?',o:['200 mcg','400 mcg','600 mcg','800 mcg'],a:1,e:'The general recommendation is 400 mcg/day folic acid; some higher-risk situations require clinician-directed dosing.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:13,q:'Why is cigarette smoking harmful during pregnancy?',o:['It dilates blood vessels','It deposits tar directly in fetal lungs','It reduces blood flow and oxygen delivery to the fetus','It delays placental detachment'],a:2,e:'Nicotine and carbon monoxide impair placental blood flow and fetal oxygen delivery.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'What is the early milk produced after birth?',o:['Progesterone','Colostrum','Estrogen','Oxytocin'],a:1,e:'Colostrum is the concentrated, immune-rich milk produced in the first days after birth.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:15,q:'Which human-milk protein binds iron and helps protect against some infections?',o:['Vitamin C','Lactoferrin','Transferrin','Hemosiderin'],a:1,e:'Lactoferrin binds iron and has antimicrobial and immune-supporting functions.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'What are the primary carbohydrates in human milk?',o:['Sucrose and lactose','Lactose and oligosaccharides','Glucose and oligosaccharides','Fructose and lactose'],a:1,e:'Lactose is the principal sugar, accompanied by diverse human milk oligosaccharides.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'A breastfeeding parent consumed 2,400 kcal/day before pregnancy. Which single total is universally required now?',o:['2,660','2,730','2,800','2,900'],a:1,e:'Current guidance adds about 330–400 kcal/day, giving roughly 2,730–2,800 here. B represents the lower estimate, but no single total is universal, so this item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:18,q:'By about 4 to 6 months, many healthy infants have:',o:['Little weight change','Doubled birth weight','Tripled birth weight','Quadrupled birth weight'],a:1,e:'Birth weight commonly doubles by about 4–6 months, though individual growth patterns vary.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'What daily vitamin D supplement is recommended for infants receiving human milk?',o:['200 IU','400 IU','600 IU','800 IU'],a:1,e:'The recommendation is 400 IU (10 mcg) daily. Formula-fed infants generally need it until intake reaches about 32 oz of fortified formula, not the source’s older 16-oz threshold.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'A full-term infant is commonly born with iron stores supporting approximately the first:',o:['Three months','Four months','Five months','Six months'],a:1,e:'Four months is the source-intended answer; in practice stores are often described as supporting roughly 4–6 months and vary by birth status.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'What measure is commonly used to assess weight status in children over age two?',o:['Balance-beam weight alone','Body mass index for age','Weight percentile alone','Bioelectrical impedance'],a:1,e:'BMI-for-age is calculated and interpreted on age- and sex-specific growth charts.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Which statement about childhood BMI is correct?',o:['BMI is not used in children','The 85th–95th percentile is obesity','The 85th–95th percentile is still called “at risk for overweight”','Classification depends on age'],a:3,e:'Child BMI is age- and sex-specific. Current terminology calls the 85th to below-95th percentile overweight; “at risk” is older wording.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:'Iron-deficiency anemia in children commonly includes all except:',o:['Irritability','Increased appetite','Inattentiveness','Decreased appetite'],a:1,e:'Increased appetite is not the expected sign; irritability, inattentiveness and reduced appetite can occur.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:24,q:'The calcium RDA during adolescence is:',o:['1,000 mg/day','1,100 mg/day','1,200 mg/day','1,300 mg/day'],a:3,e:'The RDA for ages 9–18 is 1,300 mg/day to support rapid bone growth.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'Age-related loss of lean body mass tends to cause:',o:['Energy requirements to increase','Energy expenditure to stay identical','Basal metabolism to decrease','Basal metabolism to increase'],a:2,e:'Lean tissue is metabolically active, so its loss generally lowers resting energy expenditure.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'Which is not a typical physiological trend with aging?',o:['Higher body-fat proportion','Lower bone mass','Lower lean muscle mass','Higher resting metabolic rate'],a:3,e:'Resting metabolic rate generally decreases rather than increases, partly because lean mass declines.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'After age 19, total energy expenditure decreases by exactly how many kcal/day each year for every man?',o:['5','10','15','20'],a:1,e:'Ten kcal/day per year is the source heuristic, but individual expenditure changes with activity, body composition and health. This item is unscored.',unscored:true,image:null},
{n:28,q:'Which phytochemical class is structurally similar to estrogen?',o:['Lycopene','Lutein','Catechins','Isoflavones'],a:3,e:'Soy isoflavones are phytoestrogens that can bind estrogen receptors with weaker, tissue-specific effects.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Which gastrointestinal change is more common in older adults?',o:['More gastric juice','Faster peristalsis','Reduced absorption of food-bound vitamin B12','More saliva and mucus'],a:2,e:'Atrophic gastritis and low stomach acid become more common with age and can reduce release of B12 from food; aging alone does not make this inevitable.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:30,q:'Deficiency of iron and iodine in infants and children can:',o:['Primarily damage teeth','Impair coordination only','Impair growth and cognitive development','Affect speech only'],a:2,e:'Both nutrients are important for normal growth and neurodevelopment.',unscored:false,image:null}
],
cards:[['Hyperplasia','Increase in cell number.'],['Hypertrophy','Increase in cell size.'],['Teratogen','Exposure that can disrupt prenatal development.'],['First trimester','Includes the embryonic period and early fetal period.'],['Preterm birth','Before 37 completed weeks.'],['Low birth weight','Less than 2,500 g, regardless of gestational age.'],['Healthy-BMI pregnancy gain','Generally 25–35 lb for a singleton pregnancy.'],['Pregnancy carbohydrate RDA','175 g/day.'],['Third-trimester energy','About 450 additional kcal/day on average.'],['Pregnancy protein RDA','About 71 g/day for many adults.'],['Folic acid before pregnancy','400 mcg/day from supplements and/or fortified foods.'],['Canola oil','A food source of omega-3 ALA.'],['Colostrum','Concentrated early milk rich in immune factors.'],['Lactoferrin','Iron-binding antimicrobial protein in human milk.'],['Human milk carbohydrate','Lactose plus human milk oligosaccharides.'],['Lactation energy','Generally +330 to +400 kcal/day, individualized.'],['Infant vitamin D','400 IU (10 mcg) daily when receiving human milk.'],['Birth-weight milestone','Often doubles by 4–6 months.'],['Infant iron stores','Often support roughly the first 4–6 months.'],['Child BMI','Interpreted by age and sex from age two.'],['Adolescent calcium RDA','1,300 mg/day for ages 9–18.'],['Aging and lean mass','Less lean mass tends to lower resting energy expenditure.'],['Isoflavones','Phytoestrogens found especially in soy foods.'],['Older-adult B12','Food-bound absorption may fall with atrophic gastritis or low acid.']]
};
